    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\nVW Introduces New ID. Buzz Beach for Off-Grid Fun, Here's How Much the Campervan Costs\n15 photos\nPhoto: Volkswagen UK\nIf you somehow thought that there’s no room anymore in the ID. Buzz lineup for a new addition, then you may want to hold Volkswagen’s beer, because it just introduced the Beach variant of the electric people carrier.\nThe model brings camping into the electric era, and is touching down in the United Kingdom, with pricing starting at 65,837 pounds (equal to $86,700) including VAT and OTR costs. The five-seat minivan is pretty much a flexible option for longer trips with a lot of practicality, and interested parties will have to wait until later this month to place an order, as that is when it will officially go on sale in Europe’s biggest RHD (right-hand drive) market.\nAccording to Volkswagen, the new ID. Buzz Beach can sleep two without compromising its daily usability. It features a removable bed frame with a custom-fit mattress sitting on top of it. The latter measures 2x1.2 meters (78.7x472 in), which makes it a bit larger than the standard small double bed, the company says. The model is available with seating for five, six, or seven. In the former configuration, the bed frame is hidden away in the trunk, and in the latter two, it is stored vertically behind the third row of seats.\nMind you, the bed is not the only highlight of this model, which also features a hidden foldable table and chairs set. Thus, the camping experience is elevated. In the five-seater, this set is stored under the false floor, and you can find it upright alongside the mattress in the six- and seven-seat versions.\nPhoto: Volkswagen UK Besides these, the new Volkswagen ID. Buzz Beach also features blackout curtains on the inside, as well as ventilation grilles for the front side windows for a proper night’s sleep. The new Overnight mode that can be selected by using the infotainment system, the model offers continuous off-grid climate control for up to 48 hours, regardless of the outside weather.\nThe vehicle-to-load (V2L) function is also on deck, the automaker reveals, so the zero-emission camper van can be used as a mobile power station. The 230-volt powersocket adapter is standard on Beach models, and can be used to power all kinds of things, including coffee machines, kettles, and so on, and can also be used to charge phones.\nNew exterior options are available for the Volkswagen ID. Buzz Beach, including the candy white and cherry red two-tone paint finishes, the company reveals, as well as the Stockton wheels, which measure 20 inches in diameter.\nIn addition to the new Beach, the VW ID. Buzz family comprises the Life 5-seat, Life 6-seat, Life 7-seat in the United Kingdom, as well as the 5-, 6-, and 7-seat versions of the superior Style trim level, and the sportier GTX, which sits at the top of the lineup.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nVolkswagen ID. Buzz Volkswagen ID. Buzz new cars camper van prices UK electric vehicles\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Cristian Gnaticov\nAfter a series of unfortunate events put an end to Cristian's dream of entering a custom built & tuned old-school Dacia into a rally competition, he moved on to drive press cars and write for a living. He's worked for several automotive online journals and now he's back at autoevolution after his first tour in the mid-2000s. Full profile\nUP NEXT One of the Most Technologically-Advanced Italian Superyachts Grabs Over $30M",
